.he "ar (1)                       8/12/26                       ar (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ar
- archive and library maintainer
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
ar key [ posname ] archive file ...
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Ar keeps a collection of files in one archive, which is what a library
of object modules is.
.sp
The key is a string.  Exactly one letter of it says what to do, and
any others modify it.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
KEYS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
r	Replace the named files, adding any that are not there.

.ti -10
d	Delete the named files.

.ti -10
x	Extract the named files, or all of them if none is named.

.ti -10
t	List a table of contents.

.ti -10
p	Print the named files on the standard output.

.ti -10
m	Move the named files to the end, or to where a positioning letter says.

.ti -10
q	Append without reading the whole archive first.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
MODIFIERS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
v	Verbose: say what is being done to each file.

.ti -10
u	With r, replace only files newer than the copy in the archive.

.ti -10
a	Place after the file named by posname.

.ti -10
b	Place before it.

.ti -10
i	The same as b.

.ti -10
c	Create the archive without the usual remark that it is being created.

.ti -10
l	Put temporary files in the current directory rather than in /tmp.

.ti -10
n	Do not follow the archive's own symbol conventions.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
Ar reads more than one archive format.  It looks at the magic number
in the first word and recognises the v7, v6, Whitesmith and Hitech
layouts, so an archive made by any of the compilers this machine has
carried can be listed and extracted.  It writes the v7 format.
.sp
The archive this tree builds its libraries with is not ar - the
compiler's own archiver, wslib, writes the format its linker reads.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
/tmp/v*	Temporaries, unless -l is given.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ld (1), cc (1)
